reminder that the balance requires transverse probes.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Practical readings.}
For an $n$-dimensional input a rectified first layer of width $< 2n$ is provably lossy, and no
downstream capacity recovers the loss; width $2n$ is achievable with a linear decoder and
condition number $\sqrt2$. The interval $[n,2n)$, exactly the range that dimension-counting
intuition suggests is safe, is infeasible. Structured pruning at the threshold is impossible, and
the usual pruning targets (dead units, constant units, zero rows) do not exist there. Finally,
imposing full hyperoctahedral symmetry reduces the linear stage to a single scalar, so
over-symmetrising is expensive.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
The width law concerns a single rectified layer with arbitrary weights and biases; it does not
address quantisation, unit noise, or approximate losslessness. The frame bounds are proved for
the canonical split optimum, not for every optimum. The equivariance results concern the linear
part of a layer only.

\section{Open problems}

\begin{conjecture}[Uniqueness of the bias-free optimum up to symmetry]
Let $W \in \R^{22\times 11}$ with zero bias be such that $x \mapsto \relu(Wx)$ is injective. Then
there are a permutation $\pi$ of the $22$ units, scalars $c_i>0$, and $A \in GL_{11}(\R)$ with
$w_{\pi(i)} = c_i A e_i$ for $i \le 11$ and $w_{\pi(i)} = -c_i A e_{i-11}$ for $i > 11$: every
optimal architecture is a reparametrised positive/negative split.
\end{conjecture}

The balanced-activation theorem supplies the combinatorial input --- two disjoint spanning blocks
of size exactly $11$ --- and what remains is a sign-pattern classification in finite-dimensional
linear algebra. A single injective bias-free $22 \times 11$ layer whose rows are not of this form
refutes the conjecture.

\begin{conjecture}[Stability of the width law]
For every $m \ge 22$ there is an injective rectified layer $\R^{11} \to \R^m$ whose lower frame
constant satisfies $\alpha \ge \tfrac12 + c(m-22)/m$ for an absolute $c>0$, and no injective
layer of width $m$ achieves $\alpha > \tfrac12 + C(m-22)/m$; in particular the optimal condition
number $\sqrt2$ at $m = 22$ improves continuously with excess width.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions: approximate losslessness (injectivity on a $\delta$-net, or a prescribed
bi-Lipschitz constant); whether layers required to respect the tensor factorisation of order-$k$
percepts still meet the bound $2\cdot 11^k$; least width of a lossless layer that is additionally
$S_{11}$- or $B_{11}$-equivariant as a nonlinear map; leaky rectifiers with slope $\lambda \in
(0,1)$, which are injective at width $n$, so that the threshold jumps from $n$ to $2n$ exactly at
$\lambda = 0$; and whether the invariant percept direction guaranteed in odd dimensions can be
located stably and carries interpretable meaning.

